backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 1️⃣ Data Sources & Required Columns                                      │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  | Table | Columns needed for the query | Source in schema |                 │
backend-1   | │  |-------|-----------------------------|------------------|                  │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_payment` | `rental_id`, `amount` | `required_columns.dev_payment` |  │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_rental`  | `rental_id`, `inventory_id` |                             │
backend-1   | │  `required_columns.dev_rental` |                                             │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_inventory` | `inventory_id`, `film_id` |                             │
backend-1   | │  `required_columns.dev_inventory` |                                          │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_film` | `film_id`, `rating` | `required_columns.dev_film` |          │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_film_category` | `film_id`, `category_id` |                          │
backend-1   | │  `required_columns.dev_film_category` |                                      │
backend-1   | │  | `dev_category` | `category_id`, `name` | `required_columns.dev_category`  │
backend-1   | │  |                                                                           │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  *Note*: If the revenue data lives in monthly partition tables such as       │
backend-1   | │  `dev_payment_p2022_01 … dev_payment_p2022_12`, they must be                 │
backend-1   | │  **UNION‑ALL**‑ed with `dev_payment` before the join chain. The logical      │
backend-1   | │  plan below assumes a unified view called `dev_payment_all` that already     │
backend-1   | │  contains the unioned data.                                                  │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 2️⃣ Join Path (ordered from fact → dimensions)                           │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  | Step | Join condition (exact as supplied) | Resulting columns kept |      │
backend-1   | │  |------|------------------------------------|------------------------|      │
backend-1   | │  | 1 | `dev_payment.rental_id = dev_rental.rental_id` | `amount`,            │
backend-1   | │  `dev_rental.inventory_id` |                                                 │
backend-1   | │  | 2 | `dev_rental.inventory_id = dev_inventory.inventory_id` | `amount`,    │
backend-1   | │  `dev_inventory.film_id` |                                                   │
backend-1   | │  | 3 | `dev_inventory.film_id = dev_film.film_id` | `amount`,                │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film.rating`, `dev_film.film_id` |                                     │
backend-1   | │  | 4 | `dev_film.film_id = dev_film_category.film_id` | `amount`,            │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film.rating`, `dev_film_category.category_id` |                        │
backend-1   | │  | 5 | `dev_film_category.category_id = dev_category.category_id` |          │
backend-1   | │  `amount`, `dev_film.rating`, `dev_category.name` |                          │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  All joins are **inner joins** because every payment must be linked to a     │
backend-1   | │  rental, inventory, film, and at least one genre.                            │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 3️⃣ Aggregation Logic                                                    │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  | Metric | Expression | Explanation |                                       │
backend-1   | │  |--------|------------|-------------|                                       │
backend-1   | │  | **Total Revenue** | `SUM(dev_payment.amount) AS total_revenue` | Sum of   │
backend-1   | │  `amount` (currency) per group |                                             │
backend-1   | │  | **Number of Views** | `COUNT(DISTINCT dev_rental.rental_id) AS            │
backend-1   | │  total_views` | Each rental = one “view” of a film copy. `DISTINCT`          │
backend-1   | │  protects against any accidental duplicate joins (e.g., a film belonging to  │
backend-1   | │  multiple categories). |                                                     │
backend-1   | │  | **Grouping dimensions** | `dev_film.rating` (age classification) <br>     │
backend-1   | │  `dev_category.name` (genre) | These two columns produce the required        │
backend-1   | │  breakdown. |                                                                │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 4️⃣ Final ClickHouse SQL Statement                                       │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ```sql                                                                      │
backend-1   | │ /* -------------------------------------------------                        │
backend-1   | │     Unified payment source (replace with UNION ALL                           │
backend-1   | │     of monthly tables if they exist, e.g.                                    │
backend-1   | │     SELECT * FROM dev_payment                                                │
backend-1   | │     UNION ALL SELECT * FROM dev_payment_p2022_01                             │
backend-1   | │     UNION ALL … )                                                            │
backend-1   | │     ------------------------------------------------- */                     │
backend-1   | │  WITH dev_payment_all AS (                                                   │
backend-1   | │      SELECT rental_id, amount                                                │
backend-1   | │      FROM dev_payment                                                        │
backend-1   | │  )                                                                           │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │ /* -------------------------------------------------                        │
backend-1   | │     Core query: joins + aggregation                                          │
backend-1   | │     ------------------------------------------------- */                     │
backend-1   | │  SELECT                                                                      │
backend-1   | │      f.rating                AS age_rating,                                  │
backend-1   | │      c.name                  AS genre,                                       │
backend-1   | │      SUM(p.amount)           AS total_revenue,                               │
backend-1   | │      COUNT(DISTINCT r.rental_id) AS total_views                              │
backend-1   | │  FROM dev_payment_all            AS p                                        │
backend-1   | │  INNER JOIN dev_rental      AS r ON p.rental_id = r.rental_id                │
backend-1   | │  INNER JOIN dev_inventory   AS i ON r.inventory_id = i.inventory_id          │
backend-1   | │  INNER JOIN dev_film        AS f ON i.film_id = f.film_id                    │
backend-1   | │  INNER JOIN dev_film_category AS fc ON f.film_id = fc.film_id                │
backend-1   | │  INNER JOIN dev_category    AS c ON fc.category_id = c.category_id           │
backend-1   | │  GROUP BY                                                                    │
backend-1   | │      f.rating,                                                               │
backend-1   | │      c.name                                                                  │
backend-1   | │  ORDER BY                                                                    │
backend-1   | │      f.rating,                                                               │
backend-1   | │      c.name;                                                                 │
backend-1   | │  ```                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  **Explanation of the query structure**                                      │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  | Clause | Purpose (tied to schema) |                                       │
backend-1   | │  |--------|--------------------------|                                       │
backend-1   | │  | `WITH dev_payment_all` | Guarantees a single source that can be swapped   │
backend-1   | │  with a UNION‑ALL of partition tables without changing the join logic. |     │
backend-1   | │  | `INNER JOIN … ON …` | Uses the exact join conditions listed in the        │
backend-1   | │  *join_path* section, ensuring referential integrity. |                      │
backend-1   | │  | `GROUP BY f.rating, c.name` | Groups by the two required descriptive      │
backend-1   | │  dimensions (age rating from `dev_film.rating` and genre name from           │
backend-1   | │  `dev_category.name`). |                                                     │
backend-1   | │  | `SUM(p.amount)` | Calculates total revenue (currency) per group. |        │
backend-1   | │  | `COUNT(DISTINCT r.rental_id)` | Counts unique rental transactions →       │
backend-1   | │  number of views. |                                                          │
backend-1   | │  | `ORDER BY` (optional) | Provides deterministic output ordering for        │
backend-1   | │  reporting. |                                                                │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 5️⃣ Execution Checklist (for the analyst / ETL engineer)                 │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  1. **Validate the unified payment view** – if the environment uses monthly  │
backend-1   | │  sharded tables, replace the CTE with a proper `UNION ALL` of those tables;  │
backend-1   | │  the rest of the plan remains unchanged.                                     │
backend-1   | │  2. **Confirm that every film has at least one entry in                      │
backend-1   | │  `dev_film_category`**; otherwise, films without a genre will be omitted     │
backend-1   | │  (inner join). If they must be kept, replace the last join with a `LEFT      │
backend-1   | │  JOIN` and handle `NULL` genre values.                                       │
backend-1   | │  3. **Data types** – ensure `amount` is numeric (Decimal/Float) and          │
backend-1   | │  `rating`/`name` are strings; ClickHouse will automatically cast for         │
backend-1   | │  aggregation.                                                                │
backend-1   | │  4. **Performance tips** – consider materializing the join path as a view    │
backend-1   | │  or using a `JOIN`‑engine (e.g., `JOIN` with `ANY INNER`) if the tables are  │
backend-1   | │  large, and verify that appropriate primary/key indexes exist on the join    │
backend-1   | │  columns (`rental_id`, `inventory_id`, `film_id`, `category_id`).            │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ---                                                                         │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  ### 6️⃣ Result                                                               │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  The query returns a two‑column breakdown (age rating, genre) together       │
backend-1   | │  with:                                                                       │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  * `total_revenue` – summed payment amount for all rentals of films in that  │
backend-1   | │  rating‑genre combination.                                                   │
backend-1   | │  * `total_views` – count of distinct rentals (i.e., how many times a film    │
backend-1   | │  copy was rented) for the same combination.                                  │
backend-1   | │                                                                              │
backend-1   | │  This satisfies the original analytical intent **“إجمالي إيرادات الأفلام     │
backend-1   | │  وعدد المشاهدات حسب التصنيف العمري وتصنيف الأفلام”** using only the          │
backend-1   | │  verified ClickHouse schema and join paths.                                  │
